          "entradaId": "freeman-65",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "Under the impression that the word kesitah, here rendered “pieces of money,” means a lamb, many of the ancient commentators supposed that here was an evidence of early coinage; the “pieces of money” being coins having on them the impress of a lamb. Stanley (Hist. Jewish Church, L…",
        "traduccion": {
          "resourceId": "freeman-65",
          "sourceHash": "cb5c84b340937c46262ce776648f68189e8dcfcdf9dab0326b4ebf79662a5173",
          "sourceLanguage": "en"
        }
      },
      "c-b5aab2a6017a7eb7": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-64",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "The giving of presents is far more common in the East, and has more significance, than with us. Hardly any transaction of importance can take place without a gift. The formal visits which friends make to each other are preceded by presents of fowls, sheep, rice, coffee, and other…",
        "traduccion": {
